lesson physics-year-review-6 v=1 format=review grade=6 unit=9 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Physics review: the sixth year"
  goal "Look back over the year: the tilted Earth, heat, sound waves, forces, energy and electricity, levers, falling on the moon and eclipses."

  warm-up {
    show physics.tilt-sun lat=70 s=0 easy-lat=80 hard-s=1
    show physics.heat-rod-read r=1 m=10 hard-m=5
    show physics.wave-hertz w=4 easy-w=2 hard-w=8
  }
  exercises {
    show physics.sledge-motion v=2 easy-v=1 hard-v=4
    show physics.store-missing b=1 i=1000 easy-b=0 easy-i=500 hard-b=3 hard-i=2000
    show physics.gen-water-energy m=50 h=10 easy-m=20 easy-h=5 hard-m=100 hard-h=20
    show physics.cell-lasts u=5 easy-u=2 hard-u=8
    show physics.moment-of w=6 s=3 easy-w=4 easy-s=2 hard-w=9 hard-s=4
    show physics.moondrop-speed k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=6
    show physics.phase-earth k=2 easy-k=4 hard-k=5
    show physics.eclipse-read m=1 easy-m=0
    show physics.hut-reading h=0 hard-h=1
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show physics.sledge-steady-reason v=0 hard-v=1
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show physics.cell-night-lamp v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is a check, not a test, to do on the moon at the end of the sixth year. The warm-up is the
    first question of three lessons: whether a place has the sun all day, how many beads a rod
    dropped, and the hertz of a sound on a screen. The exercises take one question each from the
    year: what a sledge does, a band of energy left to find, the joules from falling water, how long
    a cell lasts, the turning effect of a weight, a fall's speed on the moon, the Earth seen from
    the moon, which eclipse a line-up makes, and a thermometer in the hut. Anything that needs the
    way in again is worth another week with that lesson, and everything else can move on. The two
    puzzles are there to be enjoyed: the sledge puzzle is answered by the forces balancing, not by
    a bigger pull, and the lamp puzzle by working out the joules a night needs before the cells. Ask
    for the reason as well as the number.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the numbers are the smallest of each lesson: the sun at 80 degrees north in June, a rod after ten minutes, two waves on the screen, a sledge with clear arrows, the input band to find, short falls, a full Earth and an eclipse of the sun."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the numbers are the largest: the far north in December, a rod after five minutes, eight waves on the screen, a wasted band to find, water falling 20 m, a crescent or gibbous Earth, the heater high in the hut, and the hardest lamp puzzle."
  }
}
